<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1060–<a href="/years/1118">1118</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Baldwin of Boulogne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baldwin I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, earlier known as Baldwin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. In 1098 he left the main army and became ruler of Edessa, founding the first crusader state. On the death of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> in 1100 he became the first crowned king of Jerusalem, and with Italian fleets he conquered most of the coastal cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Baldwin I of Jerusalem" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baldwin_1_of_Jerusalem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 December 1110 <b>Baldwin I of Jerusalem</b> and Sigurd I of Norway took it after a siege and made it the centre of the Lordship of Sidon; Saladin captured it in 1187, German crusaders restored it in 1197, the Ayyubids destroyed it in 1249 and the Mongols…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After a siege begun in February, the forces of King <b>Baldwin I of Jerusalem</b> took the coastal city of Beirut from the Fatimid Caliphate, with the help of Bertrand of Toulouse and a Genoese fleet.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1098 <b>Baldwin of Boulogne</b> left the main army of the First Crusade and went east at the invitation of Thoros, the Armenian ruler of Edessa, who adopted him as son and heir.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-edessa">County of Edessa</a></li><li><blockquote>Duqaq joined Kerbogha's relief army at Antioch in 1098, which was defeated, and fought <b>Baldwin of Boulogne</b> on the coast in 1100.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-damascus">Seljuk Emirate of Damascus</a></li></ul></section>
